1
00:00:00,106 --> 00:00:05,039
How does a language model know that the word cat is closer to dog than to car?

2
00:00:05,452 --> 00:00:08,789
Every word is a vector, as we saw in video one.

3
00:00:09,228 --> 00:00:15,452
To compare two vectors, the model computes a single number that says how much they point the same way.

4
00:00:15,877 --> 00:00:21,329
That number is the inner product, and it is the engine inside attention.

5
00:00:22,273 --> 00:00:32,791
In this video you will compute the dot product by hand, see what it means as a picture, use its sign to detect right angles, and measure similarity with the cosine.

6
00:00:33,203 --> 00:00:39,241
You need video one on vectors and video two on matrices, especially the transpose.

7
00:00:40,999 --> 00:00:42,302
Here is the recipe.

8
00:00:42,741 --> 00:00:45,560
Take two vectors with the same number of components.

9
00:00:45,986 --> 00:00:51,544
Multiply the matching components, first with first, second with second, and so on.

10
00:00:51,983 --> 00:00:53,805
Then add up all the products.

11
00:00:54,244 --> 00:00:56,903
The result is one number, not a vector.

12
00:00:57,342 --> 00:00:58,712
This is called the dot product.

13
00:01:00,239 --> 00:01:04,907
In symbols: x dot y equals the sum over i of x i times y i.

14
00:01:05,332 --> 00:01:08,856
The letter i runs over the components, from one to n.

15
00:01:09,282 --> 00:01:17,620
In matrix language we also write x transpose y: a row times a column, which gives a one by one matrix, just a number.

16
00:01:19,126 --> 00:01:21,732
Example one, with three components.

17
00:01:22,158 --> 00:01:27,597
Let x be one, two, three, and y be four, minus five, six.

18
00:01:28,089 --> 00:01:30,908
First components: one times four is four.

19
00:01:31,333 --> 00:01:34,751
Second: two times minus five is minus ten.

20
00:01:35,190 --> 00:01:38,035
Third: three times six is eighteen.

21
00:01:38,474 --> 00:01:42,091
Add them: four minus ten plus eighteen is twelve.

22
00:01:42,517 --> 00:01:44,206
So x dot y is twelve.

23
00:01:45,093 --> 00:01:46,901
Example two, in the plane.

24
00:01:47,327 --> 00:01:48,989
Take one, one and one, two.

25
00:01:49,414 --> 00:01:52,366
One times one plus one times two is three.

26
00:01:52,792 --> 00:01:53,922
Notice something familiar.

27
00:01:54,335 --> 00:02:02,327
In video two, the row view of a matrix times a vector was exactly this: multiply matching entries and add.

28
00:02:02,765 --> 00:02:05,585
Every entry of a matrix product is a dot product.

29
00:02:07,306 --> 00:02:08,223
What does this number mean?

30
00:02:08,676 --> 00:02:09,939
Here is the picture.

31
00:02:10,364 --> 00:02:15,205
Let y lie along the horizontal axis, y equals three, zero.

32
00:02:15,630 --> 00:02:16,774
Let x be two, one.

33
00:02:17,199 --> 00:02:21,574
Shine a light from above, and x casts a shadow on the line of y.

34
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:23,476
The shadow has length two.

35
00:02:24,379 --> 00:02:30,470
Now compute x dot y: two times three plus one times zero is six.

36
00:02:30,895 --> 00:02:36,560
And the length of y times the length of the shadow is three times two, also six.

37
00:02:36,999 --> 00:02:42,784
That is the meaning: the dot product is the length of one vector times the shadow of the other on it.

38
00:02:43,222 --> 00:02:45,031
This shadow is called a projection.

39
00:02:45,973 --> 00:02:47,409
This gives a famous formula.

40
00:02:47,834 --> 00:02:55,015
x dot y equals the length of x, times the length of y, times the cosine of the angle theta between them.

41
00:02:55,454 --> 00:03:00,228
The cosine is one when the angle is zero, and zero for a right angle.

42
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:03,792
Here, length means the usual length of the arrow.

43
00:03:04,231 --> 00:03:06,598
Video six makes that idea exact.

44
00:03:08,139 --> 00:03:11,889
Let's use the formula with one, one and one, two.

45
00:03:12,328 --> 00:03:14,243
Their dot product is three.

46
00:03:14,682 --> 00:03:23,751
By Pythagoras, the length of one, one is the square root of two, and the length of one, two is the square root of five.

47
00:03:24,176 --> 00:03:30,506
So the cosine of theta is three divided by the square root of ten, about zero point nine five.

48
00:03:31,439 --> 00:03:33,992
That means the angle is about eighteen degrees.

49
00:03:34,431 --> 00:03:40,482
The two arrows point almost the same way, and the dot product told us that without any protractor.

50
00:03:40,947 --> 00:03:46,093
From the dot product we get angles, and in video six we will get lengths from it too.

51
00:03:47,806 --> 00:03:49,907
The sign of the dot product tells you a lot.

52
00:03:50,332 --> 00:03:54,176
Watch the green vector turn while the blue vector stays fixed.

53
00:03:54,601 --> 00:03:58,883
When they point roughly the same way, the dot product is positive.

54
00:03:59,295 --> 00:04:01,942
As the angle grows, the value shrinks.

55
00:04:02,859 --> 00:04:08,537
At a right angle, the dot product is exactly zero: the shadow has no length.

56
00:04:08,963 --> 00:04:14,907
Turn further, and the value becomes negative: the vectors point in opposite directions.

57
00:04:15,319 --> 00:04:20,559
When they point exactly opposite, the dot product is as negative as it can be.

58
00:04:22,106 --> 00:04:26,228
Vectors whose dot product is zero are called orthogonal.

59
00:04:26,654 --> 00:04:30,484
It is the general word for perpendicular, at a right angle.

60
00:04:30,922 --> 00:04:37,239
Check one, one and minus one, one: one times minus one plus one times one is zero.

61
00:04:37,664 --> 00:04:38,741
So they are orthogonal.

62
00:04:39,167 --> 00:04:43,010
And the zero vector is orthogonal to every vector.

63
00:04:44,539 --> 00:04:46,627
Orthogonal directions share nothing.

64
00:04:47,052 --> 00:04:50,390
If you walk purely north, you make no progress east.

65
00:04:50,802 --> 00:04:56,321
In machine learning, orthogonal features carry separate information, with no overlap.

66
00:04:56,747 --> 00:05:00,337
Keep this in mind for later videos about data and directions.

67
00:05:02,073 --> 00:05:05,410
Often we care only about direction, not about size.

68
00:05:05,836 --> 00:05:11,753
A long arrow and a short arrow that point the same way should count as equally similar.

69
00:05:12,192 --> 00:05:14,852
So we divide the dot product by the two lengths.

70
00:05:15,264 --> 00:05:17,751
The result is called cosine similarity.

71
00:05:18,673 --> 00:05:22,915
Cosine similarity is always between minus one and one.

72
00:05:23,327 --> 00:05:24,763
One means the same direction.

73
00:05:25,189 --> 00:05:26,598
Zero means orthogonal.

74
00:05:27,024 --> 00:05:28,513
Minus one means opposite.

75
00:05:28,939 --> 00:05:32,436
It is exactly the cosine of the angle between the two vectors.

76
00:05:33,986 --> 00:05:35,209
Here is a toy example.

77
00:05:35,622 --> 00:05:40,329
These are not real word vectors, just small numbers chosen to show the idea.

78
00:05:40,754 --> 00:05:44,864
Cat is zero point nine, zero point eight, zero point one.

79
00:05:45,302 --> 00:05:49,225
Dog is zero point eight, zero point nine, zero point two.

80
00:05:49,651 --> 00:05:53,614
Car is zero point one, zero point two, zero point nine.

81
00:05:54,552 --> 00:06:00,842
The cosine similarity of cat and dog is about zero point nine nine: almost the same direction.

82
00:06:01,281 --> 00:06:06,241
The cosine similarity of cat and car is only about zero point three.

83
00:06:06,667 --> 00:06:11,494
So, in this toy space, cat is much closer to dog than to car.

84
00:06:11,893 --> 00:06:15,749
Real embeddings work the same way, with hundreds of components.

85
00:06:17,506 --> 00:06:19,261
So far we used the dot product.

86
00:06:19,687 --> 00:06:25,564
Mathematicians write a more general inner product with angle brackets: x, y inside brackets.

87
00:06:25,990 --> 00:06:32,439
Any rule that pairs two vectors into one number can be an inner product, if it follows three rules.

88
00:06:33,346 --> 00:06:35,261
Rule one: it is symmetric.

89
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:38,971
The order does not matter: x with y equals y with x.

90
00:06:39,503 --> 00:06:42,442
Rule two: it is linear in each input.

91
00:06:42,867 --> 00:06:48,771
Scale or add inside one slot, and the result scales or adds the same way.

92
00:06:49,197 --> 00:06:51,192
Rule three: it is positive.

93
00:06:51,604 --> 00:06:57,362
A vector paired with itself gives a positive number, unless the vector is zero.

94
00:06:58,873 --> 00:07:01,173
Check rule three with the dot product.

95
00:07:01,599 --> 00:07:07,888
One, two, three dotted with itself is one plus four plus nine, which is fourteen.

96
00:07:08,327 --> 00:07:13,234
Squares are never negative, so the sum is positive for any non-zero vector.

97
00:07:13,686 --> 00:07:16,319
The dot product follows all three rules.

98
00:07:17,873 --> 00:07:18,710
Why does this matter?

99
00:07:19,109 --> 00:07:21,795
A vector space only knows how to add and scale.

100
00:07:22,221 --> 00:07:24,468
It has no lengths and no angles.

101
00:07:24,894 --> 00:07:26,224
The inner product adds them.

102
00:07:26,636 --> 00:07:33,817
This is our second axiom, axiom B: the inner product, the source of all geometry in this series.

103
00:07:34,256 --> 00:07:38,165
There are other inner products too, and they give different angles.

104
00:07:39,893 --> 00:07:41,329
Now the inner product at work.

105
00:07:41,754 --> 00:07:46,807
In attention, each word asks every other word: how related are we?

106
00:07:47,233 --> 00:07:50,305
The answer is a dot product, called an attention score.

107
00:07:50,717 --> 00:08:00,637
In the book Build a Large Language Model from Scratch, the word Your has the vector zero point four three, zero point one five, zero point eight nine.

108
00:08:01,573 --> 00:08:08,328
The word journey has the vector zero point five five, zero point eight seven, zero point six six.

109
00:08:08,767 --> 00:08:12,743
Their dot product is about zero point nine five four four.

110
00:08:13,182 --> 00:08:18,860
A bigger score means the two words are more related, so attention mixes in more of that word.

111
00:08:20,419 --> 00:08:22,627
Similarity search works the same way.

112
00:08:23,052 --> 00:08:32,587
To find documents or products like a query, a system compares the query vector with many stored vectors and keeps the ones with the largest cosine similarity.

113
00:08:33,012 --> 00:08:40,034
And a single neuron computes w dot x, the weights dotted with the input: a weighted sum is a dot product.

114
00:08:41,739 --> 00:08:42,258
Your turn.

115
00:08:42,683 --> 00:08:44,479
Pause the video and try this.

116
00:08:44,891 --> 00:08:49,931
Compute the dot product of two, minus one, three and one, four, two.

117
00:08:50,873 --> 00:08:52,202
Two times one is two.

118
00:08:52,641 --> 00:08:55,261
Minus one times four is minus four.

119
00:08:55,713 --> 00:08:57,495
Three times two is six.

120
00:08:57,907 --> 00:09:01,019
Add them: two minus four plus six is four.

121
00:09:01,444 --> 00:09:03,107
So the dot product is four.

122
00:09:03,532 --> 00:09:07,162
It is positive, so the vectors point roughly the same way.

123
00:09:08,706 --> 00:09:09,530
Second question.

124
00:09:09,969 --> 00:09:14,836
Are the vectors three, six and minus two, one orthogonal?

125
00:09:15,262 --> 00:09:16,113
Pause and check.

126
00:09:17,099 --> 00:09:19,732
Three times minus two is minus six.

127
00:09:20,171 --> 00:09:21,634
Six times one is six.

128
00:09:22,059 --> 00:09:24,493
Minus six plus six is zero.

129
00:09:24,932 --> 00:09:28,482
The dot product is zero, so yes, they are orthogonal.

130
00:09:28,921 --> 00:09:30,410
They meet at a right angle.

131
00:09:32,126 --> 00:09:33,522
Let's check it in NumPy.

132
00:09:33,961 --> 00:09:37,139
The at sign gives the dot product of two vectors.

133
00:09:37,565 --> 00:09:46,700
We compute our examples, the angle, the orthogonal pair, the rule checks, the toy cosine similarities, and the attention score from the book.

134
00:09:48,239 --> 00:09:52,229
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

135
00:09:52,654 --> 00:09:54,090
x dot y is twelve.

136
00:09:54,529 --> 00:09:57,388
The angle is about eighteen point four degrees.

137
00:09:57,814 --> 00:10:00,048
The orthogonal pair gives zero.

138
00:10:00,487 --> 00:10:09,529
The toy cosine similarities are zero point nine nine and zero point three, and the book's attention score is zero point nine five four four.

139
00:10:11,239 --> 00:10:11,917
In summary.

140
00:10:12,370 --> 00:10:17,755
The dot product multiplies matching components and adds them, giving one number.

141
00:10:18,181 --> 00:10:23,513
It equals the length of x times the length of y times the cosine of the angle.

142
00:10:23,939 --> 00:10:27,715
Its sign says same direction, right angle, or opposite.

143
00:10:28,154 --> 00:10:30,029
Zero means orthogonal.

144
00:10:30,959 --> 00:10:35,467
Cosine similarity divides by the lengths and keeps only direction.

145
00:10:35,893 --> 00:10:44,151
An inner product is symmetric, linear in each input, and positive: axiom B, the source of geometry.

146
00:10:44,563 --> 00:10:49,629
Next, in video six: the norm, which measures length and distance.

147
00:10:50,055 --> 00:10:50,653
See you there.

148
00:10:52,606 --> 00:10:54,428
This video was made by David R.

149
00:10:54,867 --> 00:10:58,098
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

150
00:10:58,656 --> 00:10:59,374
Thanks for watching.
